<worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm">
    <title>Math 483 Homework 3</title>

            
	<objectives>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<p>
					Define the Dirichlet kernel.
				</p>
			</li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Prove facts about the Dirichlet kernel necessary to prove convergence results for Fourier series.
                </p>
            </li>
	  	</ul>
    </objectives>
<page>

<p>
    For this assignment we need to set some notation:
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>f</m> is a piecewise continuous <m>2\pi</m> periodic function on <m>\mathbb{R}</m> that is square integrable on <m>[-\pi,\pi]</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                The <m>N</m>-th partial sum of the Fourier series for <m>f</m> is given by
                <md>
                    S_N^f(x) = \frac{a_0}{2} + \sum_{n=1}^N (a_n\cos(nx) + b_n\sin(nx)),
                </md>
                where the formulae for <m>a_n</m> and <m>b_n</m> are given as in our textbook.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</p>

<exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Show that
            <md>
                S_N^f(x) = \sum_{n=-N}^{N} c_n e^{inx},
            </md>
            where 
            <md>
                c_n = \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} f(\theta)e^{-in\theta}\,d\theta.
            </md>
            
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Show that
            <md>
                S_N^f(x) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\sum_{n=-N}^{n} f(x+\phi) e^{in\phi}\,d\phi.
            </md>
            
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>

<p>
    The previous exercise shows that 
    <md>
         S_N^f(x) = \int_{-\pi}^{\pi} f(x+\phi) D_{N}(\phi)\,d\phi,
    </md>
    where
    <md>
        D_N(\phi) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \sum_{n=-N}^{N} e^{in\phi}.
    </md>
    The function <m>D_N</m> is called the <term><m>N</m>-th Dirichlet kernel</term>. 
</p>


</page>
    



</worksheet>
